tructions for MLLM Judges L60:     5. cite50†A.5 Instructions for Explanation Generators L61:   9. cite51†B Dataset Details L62:     1. cite52†B.1 Query Selection L63:       1. cite53†Models do VQA for Query Collection. L64:       2. cite54†Screening model details. L65:       3. cite55†Answer Correctness Labeling. L66:       4. cite56†Induced-wrong set construction. L67:     2. cite57†B.2 Explanation Conditions L68:       1. cite58†Persuasion Techniques L69:     3. cite59†B.3 Explanation Text-Quality Metrics L70:       1. cite60†Metrics. L71:       2. cite61†Surface metrics separate the conditions little; entailment separates them. L72:       3. cite62†The controls behave as their design intends. L73:     4. cite63†B.4 Metrics Design L74:     5. cite64†B.5 Human Evaluation L75:       1. cite65†Criteria. L76:       2. cite66†Aggregation and agreement. L77:     6. cite67†B.6 Explanation Examples L78:   10. cite68†C More Experiment Results L79:     1. cite69†C.1 Persuasive-Rhetoric Rating L80:     2. cite70†C.2 Additional Breakdowns L81:       1. cite71†The two exceptions. L82:   11. cite72†D Generalization Details L83:     1. cite73†D.1 Datasets and Explanation Generators L84:       1. cite74†Visual grounding set. L85:       2. cite75†Multimodal explanation. L86:       3. cite76†Answers and explanations from Gemini 3 Flash. L87:     2. cite77†D.2 Image Degradation L88:       1. cite78†Blank image. L89:     3. cite79†D.3 Judging Protocols L90:       1. cite80†Multi-turn context and multi-criteria judging. L91:   12. cite81†E Prompt-Level Mitigation L92:   13. cite82†F Use of AI Assistants L93: cite83†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L94: \nL95: arXiv:2610.22631v1 [cs.CL] 15 Oct 2026\nL96: # A Plausible Explanation Is Enough to Mislead Multimodal Judges\nL97: Anglin Cai ^{†}^{†}thanks: This work was done when Anglin Cai was an intern at The Pennsylvania State University.\nL98: Affiliation: Northwestern University Email: anglincai2027@u.northwestern.edu    Zhiqi Wang Affiliation: The Pennsylvania State University Email: zhiqi.wang@psu.edu    Xiaozhi Yang Affiliation: University of Pennsylvania Email: dongwon@psu.edu    Yurun Lin Affiliation: University of Washington Email: yuchen.yang@psu.edu    Dongwon Lee Affiliation: The Pennsylvania State University Email: xiaozhi2@sas.upenn.edu    Yuchen Yang Affiliation: The Pennsylvania State University Email: lin2360@uw.edu\nL99: ###### Abstract\nL100: \nL101: Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) increasingly serve as judges in training and evaluation pipelines, often assessing answers together with explanations. Although AI judges are sensitive to textual factors, it remains unclear whether ordinary plausible explanations can sway judgments requiring visual verification. We introduce JUPE, a visual-question-answering (VQA)-style benchmark that fixes answers while varying explanations.\nL102: Across 14 MLLM judges, ordinary explanations that sound plausible mislead judges, with effects comparable on average to deliberately persuasive arguments. Plain justifications and chain-of-thought rationales reduce rejection of incorrect answers by 12.7 and 13.8 percentage points, respectively. We call this failure plausibility capture. Using signal detection theory, we find a significant mean shift in decision criterion toward acceptance, without a clear change in mean discriminability.\nL103: Explanations sway judgments across domains, explanation generators, and judging protocols, while tested mitigations offer limited, model-dependent protection. Such shifts can inflate performance estimates when judges evaluate answers with explanations.\nL104: ## 1 Introduction\nL105: \nL106: cite84†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: With the image, question, and candidate answer fixed, adding a plausible explanation changes the judge’s verdict on the incorrect answer from definitely incorrect to definitely correct.\nL107: An MLLM judge should reject an answer when the image contradicts it. Yet, as shown in Figure cite85†1 , this judgment can change when the same answer is paired with a coherent explanation. The judge is no longer evaluating only the answer against the image; it is also exposed to a textual rationale that supports the answer.\nL108: This raises a multimodal reliability question: Can plausible explanations sway multimodal judges’ decisions? This question is increasingly important as MLLMs are used as judges in training and evaluation pipelines (cite86†Chen et al., 2024 ; cite87†Xiong et al., 2025 ; cite88†Pi et al., 2025 ; cite89†Lee et al., 2024 ; cite90†Lambert et al., 2025 ).\nL109: Candidate answers often include explanations, such as chain-of-thought (CoT) rationales (cite91†Wei et al., 2022 ; cite92†Zhang et al., 2024 ) or post-hoc justifications (cite93†Lu et al., 2022 ; cite94†Turpin et al., 2023 ). While such explanations may help assess reasoning, they also introduce answer-aligned text that can compete with visual evidence.\nL110: Prior work has shown that AI judges can be influenced by textual factors such as style, framing, or persuasion (cite95†Zheng et al., 2023 ; cite96†Ye et al., 2025 ; cite97†Hwang et al., 2025 ; cite98†Tu et al., 2026 ). Our focus is how plausible explanations affect judgments that require visual verification.\nL111: To study this, we introduce JUPE (Judge Under Plausible Explanation), a controlled VQA-style benchmark. JUPE fixes candidate answers while varying only the presence and type of accompanying explanation, allowing us to isolate the effect of explanations from the answer itself. The explanations are item-specific textual rationales that support the candidate answer, instantiated as jointly generated CoT (CoT), post-hoc justifications (Plain), and persuasion-guided arguments (Persuasion).\nL112: The benchmark spans medical, chart, and hallucination settings.\nL113: Across 14 MLLM judges from five model families, we find that ordinary explanations that sound plausible are already enough to mislead judges. We call this failure mode plausibility capture: coherent answer-aligned text lowers the judge’s bar for acceptance. Plain justifications and CoT traces reduce the rejection rate of incorrect answers by 12.7 and 13.8 percentage points on average, respectively.\nL114: Arguments in the Persuasion condition generated with 41 dedicated techniques (cite99†Zeng et al., 2024 ; cite100†Hackenburg et al., 2025 ) produce a comparable 12.5-percentage-point drop. Even among wrong answers judged “definitely incorrect” without explanations, 36-39% are accepted with plausible explanations.\nL115: Plausible explanations shift judges’ decision criteria. Both correct and incorrect answers become more likely to be accepted. Using signal detection theory (SDT), we find a significant mean shift in the decision criterion toward acceptance, without a clear change in mean discriminability, the ability to distinguish correct from incorrect answers.\nL116: Overall accuracy can obscure the loss in wrong-answer detection: it falls by less than two percentage points on average, despite the 12.5-13.8-point drop in rejection of incorrect answers.\nL117: Plausibility capture persists across evaluation settings. We observe this shift across task domains, an out-of-distribution grounding set, explanation generators, image degradation, and judge rating protocols. Persuasive explanations also induce errors on easy questions that judges answer correctly on their own. We show that common mitigation strategies, such as caution prompting and solve-first verification, exhibit limited or inconsistent effectiveness.\nL118: These findings indicate that MLLM-as-judge pipelines may systematically over-accept wrong answer-explanation outputs and inflate estimates of model performance, highlighting the need to develop mitigation methods specifically designed for plausibility capture.\nL119: Our contributions are as follows:\nL120: \nL121:   * $\\bullet$\nL122: \nL123: We introduce JUPE, a benchmark isolating explanation effects on multimodal judgments.\nL124: \nL125:   * $\\bullet$\nL126: \nL127: We identify plausibility capture and use SDT to distinguish shifts in judgment criteria from changes in discriminability.\nL128: \nL129:   * $\\bullet$\nL130: \nL131: We evaluate generalization across settings and test common mitigation strategies.\nL132: ## 2 JUPE: Isolating Explanation Effects\nL133: \nL134: JUPE studies how plausible explanations influence judgments grounded in visual evidence. We instantiate this problem with VQA, using a within-item intervention design that fixes the image, question, candidate answer, and correctness label while varying only the explanation. We first formalize this judging task (§cite8†2.1 ), then describe query selection (§cite10†2.2 ) and explanation conditions (§cite11†2.3 ).\nL135: ### 2.1 Task Setup\nL136: \nL137: A VQA query is an image-question pair $(I,Q)$ drawn from a source dataset. Each query is paired with a candidate answer $A$ and a label $y\\in\\{\\text{correct},\\text{incorrect}\\}$ indicating whether $A$ matches the dataset ground truth. Under condition $c$, explanation $E_{c}$ forms a judgment item\nL138: \nL139:  | $$i_{c}=(I,Q,A,y,E_{c}).$$  |  | (1)\nL140: An MLLM judge $J$ receives $(I,Q,A,E_{c})$ and returns a four-point ordinal label $L$: definitely incorrect, probably incorrect, probably correct, or definitely correct. For binary metrics, we map the two incorrect labels to reject and the two correct labels to accept.\nL141: #### Reference-free judging.\nL142: \nL143: The judge receives the image and question but no reference answer, and is asked to assess the candidate against the visual evidence. The ground truth is used only to assign $y$. To reduce prompt artifacts, we randomize the order of the four ordinal options, require an A-D choice rather than a fixed-position label (cite101†Wang et al., 2024a ), and use an image-first layout that places the image before the answer and explanation (cite102†Deng et al., 2025 ).\nL144: ### 2.2 Query Selection\nL145: \nL146: JUPE spans three domains where correctness should be grounded in visual evidence: medical VQA, chart reasoning, and hallucination-prone visual questions. The medical domain uses VQA-RAD and PMC-VQA (cite103†Lau et al., 2018 ; cite104†Zhang et al., 2023 ); the chart domain uses ChartQA and ChartQAPro (cite105†Masry et al., 2022 ; cite106†Masry et al., 2025 ); and the hallucination domain uses HaloQuest and PRE-HAL (cite107†Wang et al., 2024b ; cite108†Huang et al., 2025 ).\nL147: We sample approximately 300 queries from each source dataset and profile them with several open-source MLLMs and GPT-5.4 with extended thinking. These profiling runs are used only for data filtering; they do not provide the candidate answers judged in JUPE. Queries that GPT-5.4 fails, or that it flags as having low-quality images, ambiguous questions, or erroneous labels, are removed.\nL148: From the retained pool, we sample 180 queries for the main set. Because easy queries rarely yield naturally wrong candidate answers, we additionally construct a 70-query induced-wrong set from easy items solved by all but at most one profiling model. These queries are paired with intentionally incorrect candidate answers to test explanation effects on questions reliably solved by the profiling models. Together, JUPE contains 250 queries.\nL149: ### 2.3 Explanation Conditions\nL150: \nL151: Table cite109†1 compares ordinary explanations with deliberately persuasive arguments and two controls, relative to a no-explanation baseline. CoT, Plain, and Persuasion are designed to provide coherent, item-specific support for the candidate answer. Here, plausibility concerns how the explanation reads as a rationale, without implying that its claims match the image. Vacuous and Mismatch supply generic or unrelated explanations to examine the role of item-specific support.\nL152: We use GPT-5.4 mini to generate all candidate answers and explanations, holding generator identity fixed (§cite33†3.4 evaluates transfer to other generators). We audit explanation quality using fluency, readability, lexical diversity, and answer entailment metrics (Appendix cite59†B.3 ), with human ratings of readability and coherence (Appendix cite64†B.5 ). For each main-set query, the generator produces a candidate answer and an associated CoT trace.\nL153: We assign $y$ by matching the candidate answer against the source-dataset ground truth, following Appendix cite52†B.1 . This yields 96 incorrect and 84 correct candidate answers. Including both allows us to distinguish changes in judgment criteria from changes in the ability to discriminate between correct and incorrect answers.\nL154: Role  | Condition  | Img.  | CoT  | Tech.  | Main $n$  | Ind. $n$\nL155: Baseline  | No explanation  | n/a  | n/a  | n/a  | 180  | 70\nL156: Plausible  | CoT  | ✓  | joint  | -  | 180  | -\nL157: Plausible  | Plain  | ✓  | -  | -  | 180  | -\nL158: Plausible  | Persuasion (VI)  | ✓  | ✓  | ✓  | 7,380  | 2,870\nL159: Plausible  | Persuasion (TO)  | -  | ✓  | ✓  | 7,380  | 2,870\nL160: Control  | Vacuous  | -  | -  | -  | 180  | -\nL161: Control  | Mismatch  | -  | shuffled^{†}  | -  | 180  | -\nL162: Total JUPE size  | 21,470\nL163: Table 1: Explanation conditions and judgment-item counts in JUPE, before judge-model replication. Main and Ind. denote the 180-query main and 70-query induced-wrong sets. Each query yields one item per non-persuasion condition and 41 per persuasion variant. Img., CoT, and Tech. denote generator access to the image, CoT trace, and persuasion-technique definition. ^{†}Mismatch uses a CoT trace from another same-domain item. VI = vision-informed; TO = text-only.\nL164: #### Plausible-explanation interventions.\nL165: \nL166: The CoT condition attaches the reasoning trace jointly generated with the candidate answer under structured reasoning instructions, such as “describe-then-answer” (cite92†Zhang et al., 2024 ). The Plain condition generates a natural post-hoc justification for the candidate answer without access to the CoT trace, persuasion instructions, or structured reasoning prompts. This condition captures ordinary item-specific answer support.\nL167: The Persuasion conditions instantiate explicit argumentation strategies. We use 41 techniques: 40 from a prior persuasion-technique taxonomy (cite99†Zeng et al., 2024 ) and one additional information-dense technique adapted from cite100†Hackenburg et al. (2025) . Each technique is generated in two variants. In Persuasion-VI, the generator receives the image and question alongside the CoT trace; in Persuasion-TO, it receives only text.\nL168: Judges always receive the image in both variants, so the comparison isolates whether visual access during explanation construction changes the explanation’s effect. The full technique list appears in Appendix cite58†B.2 .\nL169: #### Control interventions.\nL170: \nL171: The Vacuous condition supplies generic explanation-shaped text with no item-specific content, testing whether explanation presence alone shifts judge decisions. The Mismatch condition substitutes a CoT trace from another same-domain item, preserving domain style and vocabulary while removing alignment with the current image-question-answer tuple.\nL172: #### Induced-wrong set.\nL173: \nL174: For the induced-wrong set, the generator produces a plausible but clearly incorrect answer (Appendix cite52†B.1 ). We compare the no-explanation baseline with Persuasion to test whether deliberately persuasive explanations can sway judgments on these easy queries.\nL175: ## 3 Evaluation\nL176: Judge  | Acc.  | CRR $\\uparrow$  | IRR $\\downarrow$\nL177:  |  | NoExp  | Plain  | CoT  | Pers.  | NoExp  | Plain  | CoT  | Pers.\nL178: Random Predictor  | 50.0  | 50.0  | 50.0  | 50.0  | 50.0  | 50.0  | 50.0  | 50.0  | 50.0\nL179: Closed-Source Models\nL180: ---\nL181: Gemini 3 Flash  | 73.9  | 40.6  | 33.0  | 30.2  | 30.9  | 6.1  | 7.1  | 3.6  | 4.8\nL182: Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite  | 65.6  | 41.1  | 35.4  | 28.1  | 34.5  | 13.3  | 9.6  | 11.9  | 12.7\nL183: Gemini 3.1 Pro  | 65.0  | 61.5  | 67.7  | 63.5  | 64.6  | 9.5  | 10.7  | 13.1  | 12.5\nL184: GPT-5.4  | 58.9  | 35.4  | 27.1  | 27.1  | 28.5  | 9.5  | 8.3  | 8.3  | 8.2\nL185: GPT-5.4 mini  | 52.2  | 17.7  | 13.5  | 13.5  | 13.5  | 7.1  | 4.8  | 2.4  | 3.4\nL186: GPT-5.6 Luna  | 47.2  | 28.1  | 38.5  | 34.4  | 39.3  | 22.6  | 16.7  | 21.4  | 21.0\nL187: Open-Source Models\nL188: ---\nL189: InternVL3.5-14B  | 50.0  | 33.3  | 28.4  | 25.0  | 27.3  | 22.6  | 12.0  | 13.1  | 12.7\nL190: InternVL3.5-8B  | 42.8  | 41.7  | 10.4  | 12.5  | 12.7  | 19.0  | 4.8  | 6.0  | 4.3\nL191: Qwen3-VL-2B  | 49.4  | 66.7  | 27.1  | 34.4  | 33.6  | 60.7  | 23.8  | 22.6  | 22.0\nL192: Qwen3-VL-8B  | 55.6  | 40.6  | 17.7  | 12.5  | 15.5  | 20.7  | 4.8  | 2.4  | 3.8\nL193: Qwen3-VL-32B  | 50.0  | 43.8  | 26.0  | 18.8  | 21.3  | 27.4  | 9.5  | 8.3  | 12.2\nL194: Gemma 3-4B  | 38.3  | 55.4  | 38.5  | 40.6  | 38.0  | 46.3  | 35.7  | 33.7  | 31.1\nL195: Gemma 3-12B  | 40.6  | 46.9  | 21.9  | 27.1  | 27.5  | 28.6  | 10.7  | 7.1  | 11.8\nL196: Gemma 3-27B  | 40.6  | 40.6  | 30.2  | 32.3  | 31.2  | 33.3  | 17.9  | 9.5  | 16.4\nL197: Mean  | 52.1  | 42.4  | 29.7  | 28.6  | 29.9  | 23.3  | 12.6  | 11.7  | 12.6\nL198: Table 2: Judge model performance across explanation conditions. Acc. is the model’s accuracy on VQA questions. Correct Rejection Rate (CRR %) and Incorrect Rejection Rate (IRR %) are reported as absolute values for each condition: no explanation (NoExp), plain explanation (Plain), chain-of-thought (CoT), and persuasive explanation (Pers.). The darker shades of Blue or Red indicate larger rejection rate.\nL199: #### Evaluation setup.\nL200: We evaluate 14 MLLM judges from five model families: eight open-source models ranging from 2B to 32B parameters and six proprietary models. Each judge follows the reference-free prompt in §cite8†2.1 , outputs a four-point ordinal label, which is binarized into $D\\in\\{\\textsc{reject},\\textsc{accept}\\}$. The full judge list, prompts, and hyperparameters appear in Appendix cite45†A . Unless otherwise noted, evaluations use the main set.\nL201: We aggregate Persuasion over both variants and all 41 techniques, yielding 82 explanations per query; §cite27†3.3 analyzes variants and techniques.\nL202: #### Metrics.\nL203: \nL204: Let $D_{i}\\in\\{\\textsc{reject},\\textsc{accept}\\}$ be the binary decision for item $i$. We compute CRR on incorrect-candidate items $\\mathcal{I}^{-}$ and IRR on correct-candidate items $\\mathcal{I}^{+}$:\nL205: \nL206:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathrm{CRR}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\Pr(D_{i}=\\textsc{reject}\\mid i\\in\\mathcal{I}^{-}),$  |  | (2)\nL207:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathrm{IRR}$  | $\\displaystyle=\\Pr(D_{i}=\\textsc{reject}\\mid i\\in\\mathcal{I}^{+}).$  |\nL208: CRR measures correct rejection of wrong answers, while IRR measures false rejection of correct answers. We also analyze four-label verdict distributions to capture finer shifts in judge behavior. We discuss metric design in Appendix cite63†B.4 .\nL209: We further analyze each judge’s decisions using signal detection theory (SDT) (cite110†Stanislaw and Todorov, 1999 ), treating correct candidates as signals and acceptance as a “yes” response. With a hit rate of $1-\\mathrm{IRR}$ and a false-alarm rate of $1-\\mathrm{CRR}$, we quantify discriminability as $d^{\\prime}=z(\\mathrm{CRR})-z(\\mathrm{IRR})$ and the decision criterion as $c=\\frac{1}{2}[z(\\mathrm{CRR})+z(\\mathrm{IRR})]$. Here, $z$ is the standard normal quantile function.\nL210: Higher $d^{\\prime}$ indicates better discrimination, while lower $c$ indicates greater willingness to accept an answer.\nL211: Figure 2: Judgment behavior relative to no explanation. (a) Decision-criterion shift ($\\Delta c$) and (b) discriminability change ($\\Delta d^{\\prime}$) per judge (dots), with the cross-judge mean (diamonds) and item-level bootstrap 95% CI. (c) Four-label verdict shares on incorrect-candidate items, averaged over the 14 judges.\nL212: #### Research Questions.\nL213: \nL214: We organize the evaluation around four questions: RQ1 whether plausible explanations degrade judge reliability (§cite19†3.1 ); RQ2 how they shift judges’ decisions and what drives the shift (§cite22†3.2 ); RQ3 which factors modulate plausibility capture (§cite27†3.3 ); and RQ4 whether plausibility capture generalizes across deployment settings (§cite33†3.4 ). We also test mitigations (§cite38†3.5 ).\nL215: ### 3.1 Effects on Judge Reliability\nL216: \nL217: Table cite111†2 reports CRR on incorrect-candidate items and IRR on correct-candidate items for 14 judges under four explanation conditions.\nL218: #### Ordinary plausible explanations and deliberate persuasion produce similar average rejection declines.\nL219: On incorrect-candidate items, plausible explanations reduce rejection rate by comparable margins: 12.7 pp under Plain, 13.8 pp under CoT, and 12.5 pp under Persuasion. Persuasion is thus no more effective than a plain justification on average. Open-source judges show larger effects (20 pp on average under each condition), whereas four of the six proprietary judges drop by 4-13 pp and the other two rise under all three conditions: Gemini 3.1 Pro and GPT-5.6 Luna.\nL220: We discuss these two exceptions further in §cite22†3.2 .\nL221: #### The shift reaches confident rejections.\nL222: The share of “definitely incorrect” verdicts falls from 26.9% without explanation to 16.5-18.7% with plausible explanations (Figure cite112†2 c). Of the candidates a judge labels “definitely incorrect” without explanation, 36-39% are accepted when the same candidate comes with a plausible explanation, and 23% are labeled “definitely correct”, compared with 10% and 7% under Vacuous, which shares the prompt format and randomized option order but offers no answer support.\nL223: ### 3.2 How Explanations Shift Judgments\nL224: #### Explanations also reduce rejection of correct answers.\nL225: On correct-candidate items, plausible explanations also lower rejection rate, on average by 10.7 pp under Plain, 11.6 pp under CoT, and 10.7 pp under Persuasion, with declines observed in 12-13 of the 14 judges. As with CRR, the change is concentrated in open-source judges, which start from a far higher IRR (32.3% vs. 11.3%). The similar CRR and IRR declines raise a question: do explanations improve discrimination, or change the criterion used to judge answers?\nL226: Figure cite112†2 decomposes this shift: signal-detection criterion and discriminability (a-b) and four-label verdict shares on incorrect-candidate items (c). Per-judge values, label shares, and verdict transitions are in Appendix cite70†C.2 (Tables cite113†8 and cite114†9 , Figure cite115†8 ).\nL227: #### Judgment criteria shift without a clear mean change in discriminability.\nL228: \nL229: All three explanation conditions shift the mean criterion toward acceptance by similar amounts ($\\Delta c=-0.39$, $-0.45$, and $-0.39$ under Plain, CoT, and Persuasion; 95% CIs exclude zero), with this direction observed in 12 of 14 judges (Figure cite112†2 a). Mean $\\Delta d^{\\prime}$ ranges from $+0.04$ to $+0.09$, with CIs spanning zero; discriminability rises for 7-8 judges and falls for the rest (Figure cite112†2 b).\nL230: #### Overall accuracy can conceal worse error detection.\nL231: \nL232: Across 14 judges, judgment accuracy drops by less than 2 pp (58.3% to 56.4-56.7%) while CRR falls by 12.5-13.8 pp. For Gemma 3-27B, accuracy rises by 1.7-6.7 pp despite an 8.3-10.4 pp CRR decline. More frequent acceptance of correct answers can thus mask increased acceptance of wrong ones (Appendix cite63†B.4 ).\nL233: #### Controls suggest a role for item-specific answer support.\nL234: Vacuous explanations, which add explanation-shaped text without item-specific content, produce a small shift toward rejection ($\\Delta c=+0.08$, 95% CI $[+0.03,+0.12]$), opposite to plausible explanations. Mismatch explanations shift all 14 judges toward rejection (mean $\\Delta c=+0.95$; Figure cite112†2 a) and raise IRR by 30.7 pp (Appendix cite70†C.2 , Table cite116†7 ). These contrasts suggest that item-specific answer support contributes to the criterion shift.\nL235: The two exceptions in §cite19†3.1 , Gemini 3.1 Pro and GPT-5.6 Luna, differ from their own Vacuous criterion by at most 0.07 under plausible explanations, versus 0.12-1.01 for the other judges. Pro responds little to explanation content, whereas Luna gains discriminability; Luna nonetheless shows capture on the independent dataset in §cite33†3.4 . Appendix cite71†C.2 details both cases.\n"
